<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esus was a Gaulish god named by the Roman poet Lucan, together with Teutates and Taranis, as receiving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>. He appears on the Pillar of the Boatmen, a 1st-century monument from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, as a man cutting a tree, and on a relief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. Little else is known of his cult, and his relationship to other Celtic gods is uncertain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esus.jpg" alt="Esus" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clio20 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Pilier_des_Nautes_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
